---
import SimplePageLayout from "../layouts/SimplePageLayout.astro";

const formats = [
  { name: "URL", href: "/url-qr-code-generator" },
  { name: "WiFi", href: "/wifi-qr-code-generator" },
  { name: "vCard", href: "/vcard-qr-code-generator" },
  { name: "Email", href: "/email-qr-code-generator" },
  { name: "SMS", href: "/sms-qr-code-generator" },
  { name: "Phone", href: "/phone-qr-code-generator" },
  { name: "PDF link", href: "/pdf-qr-code-generator" },
  { name: "Google Forms", href: "/google-forms-qr-code-generator" },
  { name: "Facebook", href: "/facebook-qr-code-generator" },
  { name: "Instagram", href: "/instagram-qr-code-generator" },
  { name: "LinkedIn", href: "/linkedin-qr-code-generator" },
];
---
<SimplePageLayout
  title="About QR Workbench"
  description="What QR Workbench is, how the QR encoding actually works, and why it runs entirely in your browser."
  eyebrow="About"
  h1="What QR Workbench actually does"
  intro="A free, browser-based QR code generator for eleven formats, plus a bulk workspace that turns a spreadsheet into a ZIP of correctly named codes. No signup, nothing uploaded to a server."
>
  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <p>QR Workbench turns whatever you type — a link, a WiFi password, a contact card — into a real, scannable QR code, instantly, in your browser. Pick a format, fill in the fields, download an SVG or PNG. Need a few hundred or a few thousand at once instead of one? The <a href="/bulk-qr-code-generator">bulk workspace</a> takes a CSV and generates a ZIP.</p>

  <h2>How the encoding actually works</h2>
  <p>Every code is generated with a real QR encoding library, not a decorative pattern — the same one used across millions of production sites. You control the error correction level (L through H), size, margin, and colors, and the result is a standards-compliant QR code every time.</p>
  <p>Format-specific generators don't just encode plain text. WiFi codes are built as a proper <code>WIFI:T:...;S:...;P:...;;</code> payload, vCards follow the vCard 3.0 contact-card format, and email/SMS/phone codes use the standard <code>mailto:</code>, <code>sms:</code>, and <code>tel:</code> schemes that phones already know how to handle. That's the difference between a code that scans into "a pile of text" versus one that opens the right app with the right fields filled in.</p>

  <h2>Formats supported today</h2>
  <ul>
    {formats.map((f) => <li><a href={f.href}>{f.name}</a></li>)}
  </ul>
  <p>Google Forms, Facebook, Instagram, LinkedIn, and PDF generators encode a link you provide — QR Workbench doesn't host, verify, or have any relationship with those platforms beyond generating a code that points to a URL you already control.</p>

  <h2>Why static QR</h2>
  <p>A static QR code has its destination encoded directly into the pattern. There's no redirect server in between — which means no ongoing dependency on QR Workbench (or anyone else) to keep working, and no expiry date. The tradeoff is that a static code can't be edited after it's printed; if the destination needs to change, you generate a new one. For most use cases — a WiFi sign, a business card, a product link — that tradeoff is the right one.</p>

  <h2>Free generators, paid bulk</h2>
  <p>The eleven individual generators above are free, permanently, with no account and no limit. The <a href="/bulk">bulk workspace</a> — CSV upload, column mapping, batch generation, ZIP export — is free to try up to 50 codes per batch; a one-time payment ($7 for a single batch pass, $39 for unlimited lifetime access) unlocks larger batches via a license key, no account or subscription required. See the <a href="/pricing">pricing page</a> for details.</p>

  <h2>Privacy by construction</h2>
  <p>Every generator — individual and bulk — runs entirely in your browser's own JavaScript. There's no server-side component receiving what you type, so there's nothing to log even if we wanted to. Read the <a href="/privacy-policy">Privacy Policy</a> for the specifics.</p>

  <h2>What's still being built</h2>
  <p>Printable label sheets (A4/Letter, rows and columns of codes with captions) are in progress — the <a href="/labels">Label Generator</a> page is a placeholder until that ships. QR Workbench intentionally isn't trying to become a dynamic-QR platform, an analytics dashboard, or an account-based SaaS — the free generator and the bulk workflow are the whole product.</p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <p>Get in touch through the <a href="/contact">contact page</a> — bug reports, format requests, and general feedback are all welcome.</p>
</SimplePageLayout>
